alue "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552112} :answer-field/id #uuid "cff42165-0565-50e9-9c24-d18be82e3972" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46fec928-7e8c-5465-b67b-b3ef68641ebc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75114" :question/problem "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the  three given elementary row operations. \n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$"}]}
